[273.075512, "o", "  ⚙ bash ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/ 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 11 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/ 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/ 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The bundled pip wheel is there. Let me use `ensurepip` to reinstall pip f…\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ That didn't fix it — it thought pip was already installed. The issue is t…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&"]
[273.075641, "o", "…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick smoke test to confirm package installation works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\npip is fully working now.\r\n\r\n**The problem:** The `pip` package was corrupted. The `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata folder existed in `site-packages`, making Python think pip was installed, but the actual `pip/` package directory (containing the Python code like `pip/_internal/cli/main.py`) was missing. This caused `ModuleNotFoundError: N"]
[273.075679, "o", "o module named 'pip'` when trying to run pip.\r\n\r\n**The fix:** I removed the stale `.dist-info` directory and reinstalled pip from the bundled wheel using `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which restored the complete pip package.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=17 surface=3a3dccff0421\r\n"]
